      <h1>Internal Resistance &amp; Conductance Testing</h1>
      <p class="muted">Battery Testing Methods · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>

      <div class="box">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> A conductance tester sends a small <strong>AC signal</strong> through the battery
        and measures how easily current passes — <strong>conductance</strong>, the inverse of internal resistance. Because
        resistance rises with age (sulfation, corrosion), the tester uses the reading to <strong>estimate CCA and state
        of health</strong>. It is fast and non-destructive, but it is an estimate, not the true load test it replaced.
      </div>

      <h2>Conductance vs a true load test</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Conductance test</th><th>Load test</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Method</strong></td><td>Small AC signal, measures conductance</td><td>Heavy resistive load (~½ CCA for ~15 s)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>What it reads</strong></td><td>Internal resistance → estimates CCA</td><td>Actual voltage under load</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Speed / battery state</strong></td><td>Seconds; can run on a discharged battery</td><td>Slower; needs a full charge first</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Nature of result</strong></td><td>Estimate</td><td>Direct measurement</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why resistance tracks health</h2>
      <p>
        A healthy battery has low internal resistance — a few milliohms. Aging raises it: sulfation coats the
        plates and blocks current, corrosion adds resistance at the grid, and a low state of charge also raises it.
        Because a higher resistance means the battery can deliver less current (and therefore less CCA), the
        conductance reading maps to a CCA estimate. That correlation is strong but not perfect, which is why the
        number is an estimate, not a measurement.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read the result</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Conductance near the rated CCA</strong> — the battery is likely healthy.</li>
        <li><strong>Conductance well below the rated CCA</strong> — capacity or cranking ability has degraded.</li>
        <li><strong>Requires full charge for a load test</strong> — a partially-charged battery reads low on either test.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="box">
        <p><strong>My position:</strong> The conductance tester won because it is fast and safe, but the skill is knowing
        it reads resistance and <em>estimates</em> CCA — a proxy that is good enough for triage, not a courtroom-grade
        measurement.</p>
        <p><strong>What drives this:</strong> For most decisions — is this battery still good — the estimate is sufficient. But when a
        warranty or a large purchase turns on the exact number, the load test is the one to trust.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

